Q: Spring Boot @Async annotation and CompletableFuture A: <p>@Async annotation is about configuring a task executor (or use default).</p>
<pre><code>    @Async(&quot;yourThreadPoolTaskExecutor&quot;)
    public CompletableFuture&lt;T&gt; doWork(Object arg){
        // your code;
        T t;

        return CompletableFuture.completedFuture(t);
    }
</code></pre>
<p>Example: How to configure a custom task executor is shown below.</p>
<pre><code>@Configuration
@EnableAsync
public class AsyncConfiguration {

    @Bean(name=&quot;yourThreadPoolTaskExecutor&quot; )
    TaskExecutor getThreadPoolTaskExecutor(){

        ThreadPoolTaskExecutor ex = new ThreadPoolTaskExecutor();
        ex.setQueueCapacity(20);
        ex.setCorePoolSize(2);
        ex.setMaxPoolSize(6);
        ex.setThreadNamePrefix(&quot;async-thread-&quot;);
        //ex.initialize();
        return ex;
    }
}
</code></pre>

Q: jQuery change hash (fragment identifier) while scrolling down page A: <p><a href="https://stackoverflow.com/a/79883790/5008962">This answer here</a> demonstrates how to do that without jQuery using modern APIs like the history API and URL constructor</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>window.addEventListener('load', () =&gt; {
    const headings = document.querySelectorAll('h1[id],h2[id],h3[id],a[id]');

    document.addEventListener('scroll', () =&gt; {
        headings.forEach(elementWithAnchor =&gt; {
            const rect = elementWithAnchor.getBoundingClientRect();
            if (rect.top &lt;= 0 || rect.top &gt; 150) {
                return;
            }

            const newHash = `#${elementWithAnchor.id}`;
            if (window.location.hash == newHash) {
                return false;
            }

            history.replaceState(null, null, new URL(newHash, window.location));
        });
    });
});
</code></pre>

Q: How To Update URL Hash On Scroll (With Table Of Contents) A: <p>Some improvements on @James Marks <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/a/58127673">answer</a>:</p>
<ul>
<li><p>Instead of <code>name</code>  you can use <code>id</code> directly that you also use to specify what URLs/elements should be scrolled to/at. You can specify/change the selector to clarify where this is used/applied.</p>
</li>
<li><p>uses not only the modern HistoryAPI to replace the state, but also...</p>
</li>
<li><p>use the URL constructor for adding the hash. E.g. you do not need to remove the hash from the current URL then, this is done implicitly/automatically.</p>
</li>
<li><p>also adjusted the code style for more &quot;early return&quot; if conditions</p>
</li>
<li><p>added the suggested guard so that changes do not happen too often (if nothing actually changed)</p>
</li>
</ul>
